<!--Post: 3070--><!--Time: 1104420552-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3070"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:dammitslicky@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Cougar<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Cougar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 7:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Yesterday a friend and I took my two year old to the Burger King on East Harry. For whatever reason this BK is decorated with war memorabilia: glass cases with helmets, patches, various military gear and quite a few displays with reference to WWII aircraft. I couldn&#39;t help but notice an old gent sitting to the left of us. His face looked unbelievably like the Dennis McKenzie rendering of old BTK! I am dead serious. He was wearing an old houndstooth coat, a ball cap with golf balls on it, blue jeans, and cheap sneakers. He stared at me as much as I was staring at him, making eye contact several times. His gaze was steady and sharp - not a feeble old man&#39;s &#39;look&#39;. I still cannot believe how much he looked like the sketch. My friend said that I should wait until he leaves and then get his cup for LE to swab but I told her that my guts were telling me that he wasn&#39;t going to leave until we did. And I was right. He took a long time, going from display to display, looking at the war memorabillia, carrying his cup the whole time. Honestly, if I had not had my toddler with me I would have spoke to him, saying that he looked familiar to me, just to see what he would say. We wound up leaving the restaraunt and waiting in the car to see if he left on foot or in a vehicle, and then, which way he went. We never saw him leave. After 15 minutes or so we drove away.  <BR> <BR>Just wanted to share. It was a strange experience for sure. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3070-->
<!--Post: 3078--><!--Time: 1104432394-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3078"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 10:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi cougar <BR> <BR>Thanks for sharing you experience... <BR> <BR>I was thinking that maybe you could ask one of the employed at the BK if that know this guy and if he is a regular and maybe get som info on him? <BR> <BR>I think BTK is a man of very strict routines, so he might be a regular at that specific BK, IF ideed it is BTK, wich I doubt, but then again who knows?  <BR>just a thought. <BR> <BR>skov<!--/Text--></td></tr>
